//! Call system — native Slack huddles.
//!
//! # Status: working since 2026-09-30, in daily use.
//!
//! An earlier version of this module contained a complete signaling client
//! that exchanged SDP and ICE candidates over the RTM WebSocket with
//! `call_start` / `call_sdp_update` / `call_ice_candidate` messages. **That
//! protocol does not exist.** It was a plausible but invented reconstruction,
//! and it could not work for two independent reasons:
//!
//!   1. None of those message types is accepted by Slack's RTM, and the
//!      `call_id` was generated by the client while it is the server that
//!      assigns it.
//!   2. Slack huddles are not peer-to-peer. They are multi-party and hosted by
//!      Slack, so the media plane goes through an SFU even with only two
//!      participants. An SDP offer sent "to the peer" has no recipient.
//!
//! It was removed because code that pretends to implement a protocol is worse
//! than no code at all: it hides the fact that the protocol is still to be
//! discovered.
//!
//! # What we know now
//!
//! The protocol was worked out from the webapp bundles: see
//! `docs/huddle-protocol.md`. In short, huddles are the **Amazon Chime SDK**
//! (`amazon-chime-sdk-js`, Apache-2.0), so the signaling and the media plane
//! are already documented upstream. Slack only acts as the authenticator:
//! `rooms.join` returns `call.free_willy.{meeting,attendee}`, that is, the AWS
//! `CreateMeeting`/`CreateAttendee` responses, and it does accept an `xoxc`
//! token.
//!
//! `screenhero.rooms.create`, which this module still calls, belongs to the
//! previous generation. The official client uses `rooms.join`.
//!
//! What this module holds is the diagnostic probe, which prints and blocks;
//! the call an interface drives lives in [`live`].

